I found the key part of the chat template:

```
{%- if add_generation_prompt %}
    {{- '<|im_start|>assistant\n' }}
    {%- if enable_thinking is defined and enable_thinking is false %}
        {{- '<think>\n\n</think>\n\n' }}
    {%- else %}
        {{- '<think>\n' }}
    {%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
```

When `enable_thinking` is not defined or is True, the template adds `<think>\n` at the end of the generation prompt, causing the model to start thinking.


When `enable_thinking=False`, it closes the thinking block immediately with `<think>\n\n</think>\n\n`, forcing non-thinking output. Since GRPOTrainer sets this internally, the simplest approach is to modify the tokenizer's chat_template after loading to always use the closed thinking variant.

Modifying train_grpo.py... Modifying the tokenizer template...

